  "name": "Question 20",
  "problem": "$\\,$ $P(x):$ $\\sqrt{x}-2=0\u21d2{x}^{2}\\in N$\n\nGiven that the above predicate is defined over the universal set $Z,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The truth set of $P(x)$ is $\u2205.$\n- The truth set of $P(x)$ is $Z.$\n- $\u2203x\\in Z,\\,P(x).$",
  "worked_solution": "The *existential quantification* of a predicate $P(x),$ defined over the universal set $U,$ transforms this predicate into a statement that we write as\n\n$$\n\u2203x\\in U,\\,P(x).\n$$\n\nThis statement is true if the predicate $P(x)$ is true for a single element $x\\in U,$ i.e. if the truth set ${T}_{P}$ of the predicate is not empty:\n\n$$\n{T}_{P}\\ne \u2205\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that:\n\n- the truth set of \"$\\sqrt{x}-2=0$\" is the set ${4},$ and\n\n- the truth set of \"${x}^{2}\\in N$\" is $Z\u2216{0}.$\n\nSince $A\u21d2B\u2261\u00acA\u2228B,$ the truth set of our predicate is \n\n$$\n{T}_{P}=\\overset{\u2015}{{4}}\\cup (Z\u2216{0})=(Z\u2216{4})\\cup (Z\u2216{0})=Z.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The truth set is *not* empty.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, ${T}_{P}=Z.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since ${T}_{P}$ is *not* empty, the statement $\u2203x\\in Z,\\,P(x)$ is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"",
